# Finance & AI Briefing

**FIN-AI-2026-45** =C2=B7 9 November 2026 =C2=B7 Covering 2=E2=80=938 Novembe=
r 2026

---

### 1. WHAT CHANGED

**US banking supervision is putting AI autonomy and consequences at the centr=
e of examination planning.**

Reporting published on 5 November describes an Office of the Comptroller of t=
he Currency supervisory framework for assessing banks=E2=80=99 use of artific=
ial intelligence in normal-course business activities. Examinations are to re=
flect the bank=E2=80=99s size, complexity and risk profile, alongside the con=
sequences, autonomy and complexity of individual AI applications. The report =
does not specify the framework=E2=80=99s issuance date.[1](https://my.silobre=
aker.com/v2/document/5_2281859248377823232)

The structural implication is a move beyond treating AI as one technology cat=
egory. A drafting assistant and an agent permitted to initiate financial acti=
ons create different control exposures, even when they use the same underlyin=
g model. An inventory organised only by vendor or model name cannot explain t=
hose differences.

For banking management, the resulting exposure spans operational losses, cust=
omer redress and the evidence available after a disputed decision. Model accu=
racy remains relevant, but authority, escalation and traceability become equa=
lly important parts of the control record.

=E2=86=92 If you do one thing this week: Audit the percentage of production A=
I applications whose inventory records both permitted actions and the account=
able business owner.



Independent Community Bankers of America



### 2. MODEL RACE

*Evaluating foundation model releases through enterprise cost, security, and =
data sovereignty.*

#### **THINKING MACHINES LAB: Inkling 2 Small**



New model and architecture developments



Released on 5 November, the 594-billion-parameter model supports text and ima=
ges through Tinker. Weights remained closed pending safety tests, with Apache=
 2.0 release planned. Reporting supplies neither token pricing nor residency =
commitments; its intelligence benchmark result does not establish reduced hal=
lucination rates in banking workflows.[2](https://my.silobreaker.com/v2/docum=
ent/5_2281861551822471172)&#x20;

Read more



#### **RICURSIVE INTELLIGENCE: Ripple-65B Preview**



Open-weight coding architecture



Released on 4 November with open weights, Ripple-65B Preview targets coding a=
nd hardware-efficient execution on Nvidia systems. The developer reports codi=
ng strength, but independent evaluations remain absent. Reporting provides no=
 hosted token tariff, deployment-region commitments or hallucination measurem=
ents, leaving those procurement questions unresolved for banking buyers.[2](h=
ttps://my.silobreaker.com/v2/document/5_2281861551822471172)&#x20;

Read more



#### **ZHIPU: GLM 5.3 adoption at Revolut**



LLM Meter =E2=80=94 Week of Nov 5, 2026



Revolut reportedly made self-hosted GLM 5.3 its default coding model for appr=
oximately 2,000 engineers. This is an adoption milestone, not a launch announ=
cement. The model is also available through Amazon Bedrock; reporting does no=
t establish comparable token pricing, deployment jurisdiction or measured red=
uctions in hallucinations.[3](https://my.silobreaker.com/v2/document/5_228185=
9138872934409)&#x20;

Read more



### 3. KEY DEVELOPMENTS

*Verifiable industry milestones, major peer deployments, and regulatory frame=
work shifts.*

#### **BBVA: Voice agents enter production testing**



BBVA puts AI agents=E2=80=99 controls to the test



Reporting on 3 November described production pilots in Peru and Mexico where =
BBVA said voice agents resolved close to 90% of interactions. The published a=
ccount did not explain the resolution measure. It provides a deployment indic=
ator, but not evidence of equivalent customer outcomes or complaint performan=
ce.[4](https://my.silobreaker.com/v2/document/5_2281854186725310466)&#x20;

Read more



#### **SOUTH KOREA: Network-separation rules face phased removal**



Fighting AI with AI



Authorities plan to phase out financial-sector network-separation requirement=
s, according to reporting on 6 November. Institutions with greater preparedne=
ss would move first, while authorities develop a replacement security framewo=
rk. The change concerns access to technologies including AI; it is not an unc=
onditional removal of financial institutions=E2=80=99 security obligations.[5=
](https://my.silobreaker.com/v2/document/5_2281860881455251464)&#x20;

Read more



#### **BDC: Mission AI and AI Studio launch**



BDC launches two entrepreneur-led initiatives



BDC launched Mission AI and AI Studio on 2 November to support business adopt=
ion and implementation. Its disclosure also reported investments in more than=
 300 Canadian AI companies, directly and indirectly, representing nearly $800=
 million as of September. That portfolio figure is not a newly announced week=
ly commitment.[6](https://my.silobreaker.com/v2/document/5_228185253658545356=
9)&#x20;

Read more



#### **DEUTSCHE BANK: Private Bank AI Academy expands training**



Deutsche Bank establishes AI academy for private bankers



The Private Bank=E2=80=99s AI Academy was reported on 6 November as available=
 to approximately 3,500 employees internationally, with expansion planned to =
more than 20,000. The figures distinguish current availability from intended =
reach. The report does not provide a programme budget or quantified evidence =
of resulting productivity improvements.[7](https://my.silobreaker.com/v2/docu=
ment/5_2281861299845464071)&#x20;

Read more



### GROUP FOCUS

*Key internal announcements and published deployments across BNP Paribas Grou=
p and entities.*

- **Markets operations reconciliation** =E2=80=94 In a published executive ac=
count on 6 November, BNP Paribas described using AI to reconcile structured a=
nd unstructured information. The example concerned recognising different vers=
ions of a counterparty=E2=80=99s name across a booking system and an email. T=
his documents a specific operational application rather than a group-wide dep=
loyment count or quantified financial return.[8](https://my.silobreaker.com/v=
